<section id="comparison" class="comparison-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="badge badge-power">Thermodynamic Comparison</span>
      <h2>Petrol (Spark Ignition) vs Diesel (Compression Ignition)</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">
        A fundamental mechanical engineering comparison of operating cycles, ignition principles, and combustion dynamics.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Interactive Comparison Cards -->
    <div class="grid-2" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="eng-card comp-card petrol-card">
        <div class="comp-card-badge">PETROL / GASOLINE (SI)</div>
        <h3 class="comp-engine-title">Four-Stroke Spark Ignition (Otto Cycle)</h3>
        <ul class="comp-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Fuel Type:</strong> Petrol / Gasoline (volatile, high octane rating ~91–98 RON, resists pre-ignition).</li>
          <li><strong>Intake Charge:</strong> Homogeneous mixture of air and vaporized fuel drawn into cylinder during intake.</li>
          <li><strong>Compression Ratio:</strong> Moderate (r = 8.0:1 to 11.5:1) limited strictly by fuel knock / auto-ignition.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignition Mechanism:</strong> Electric discharge arc from a spark plug near TDC (20,000–30,000 V).</li>
          <li><strong>Combustion Model:</strong> Approximately constant-volume heat addition (V &approx; constant).</li>
          <li><strong>Load Governing:</strong> Quantity governing via throttle plate regulating total intake mixture mass.</li>
          <li><strong>Thermal Efficiency:</strong> Moderate indicated efficiency (&eta; &approx; 28% - 35%).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="eng-card comp-card diesel-card">
        <div class="comp-card-badge diesel-badge">DIESEL (CI)</div>
        <h3 class="comp-engine-title">Four-Stroke Compression Ignition (Diesel Cycle)</h3>
        <ul class="comp-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Fuel Type:</strong> Diesel fuel (heavier hydrocarbon distillates, high cetane rating ~45–55 for quick auto-ignition).</li>
          <li><strong>Intake Charge:</strong> Pure ambient air only is inducted; fuel is never present during intake or compression.</li>
          <li><strong>Compression Ratio:</strong> High (r = 15.0:1 to 22.0:1), producing air temperatures exceeding 600 °C at TDC.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignition Mechanism:</strong> Self-ignition (Compression Ignition) when atomized fuel is injected into hot air (no spark plug).</li>
          <li><strong>Combustion Model:</strong> Approximately constant-pressure heat addition (P &approx; constant or Dual cycle).</li>
          <li><strong>Load Governing:</strong> Quality governing (unthrottled air intake; fuel mass injected varies with load).</li>
          <li><strong>Thermal Efficiency:</strong> High indicated efficiency (&eta; &approx; 38% - 46%) due to higher compression ratio.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Detailed Specification Table -->
    <div class="eng-card table-card">
      <div class="eng-card-header">
        <span class="eng-card-title">Comprehensive Engineering Comparison Matrix</span>
        <span class="badge badge-intake">10 Technical Dimensions</span>
      </div>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="eng-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Technical Parameter</th>
              <th>Petrol (Spark Ignition) Engine</th>
              <th>Diesel (Compression Ignition) Engine</th>
              <th>Engineering Reason / Justification</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Working Cycle</strong></td>
              <td>Otto Cycle (Constant-Volume)</td>
              <td>Diesel / Dual Combustion Cycle</td>
              <td>Rapid flame propagation in petrol allows near-instantaneous heat addition at TDC.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Ignition Method</strong></td>
              <td>Spark Plug Electric Arc</td>
              <td>Compression Heat Auto-ignition</td>
              <td>Diesel auto-ignition temperature is lower (~210 °C) and is reached by high compression of air.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Compression Ratio (CR)</strong></td>
              <td>8:1 to 11.5:1</td>
              <td>15:1 to 22:1</td>
              <td>Higher CR in petrol causes destructive detonation (knocking). Diesel requires high CR to exceed self-ignition temp.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Air Intake Mechanism</strong></td>
              <td>Throttled air-fuel mixture</td>
              <td>Unthrottled pure ambient air</td>
              <td>Petrol requires near stoichiometric air-fuel ratio (~14.7:1); diesel runs lean across all operating loads.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Fuel Injection Pressure</strong></td>
              <td>3 to 200 bar (PFI to GDI)</td>
              <td>1,500 to 2,500 bar (Common Rail)</td>
              <td>Diesel must atomize micro-droplets instantly into high-density compressed air at end of stroke.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Peak Cylinder Pressure</strong></td>
              <td>45 to 70 bar</td>
              <td>120 to 190 bar</td>
              <td>Diesel operates under much higher compression and combustion pressures, requiring heavier structures.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Engine Weight-to-Power</strong></td>
              <td>Low (lightweight aluminium)</td>
              <td>Higher (reinforced block & crank)</td>
              <td>Diesel engine block, connecting rods, and crankshaft must endure 2.5× higher structural shock loads.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Rotational Speed (RPM)</strong></td>
              <td>High (typically 4,000–8,000 RPM)</td>
              <td>Moderate (typically 1,800–4,500 RPM)</td>
              <td>Heavy reciprocating parts and slower diesel combustion diffusion limit maximum engine rotational speed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Brake Thermal Efficiency</strong></td>
              <td>25% to 35%</td>
              <td>35% to 46%</td>
              <td>Diesel thermal efficiency is higher because higher compression ratio directly yields greater expansion ratio.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Primary Applications</strong></td>
              <td>Passenger cars, motorbikes, small aircraft</td>
              <td>Commercial trucks, buses, ships, locomotives, generators</td>
              <td>Petrol favors high power-to-weight and smooth acceleration; diesel favors high low-end torque and fuel economy.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
